================================================================================
Run:      2026-10-09 09:36-09:39 NZ (box local; both device clocks set to box local time
          before the run, so device and host stamps are directly comparable)
Bench:    tb277, consoles u2, u3, u5; Test Engineer bidhanc@bidhanc-dl
Tester:   bench-runner
Graded:   tester
Case:     AWP-25167, objective "CR56263 regression -- set a mirror destination to
          `interface none` and confirm the system stays stable (was an NSM crash)", 5 steps
Build:    swi_c  sin_x96056-20260921-9 (x960-tb277.rel, build Mon Sep 21 11:25:03 UTC 2026)
          swi_b  sin_x96056-20260921-9
Configs:  swi_c.cfg, swi_b.cfg (running-config after setup; saved to startup: no)

VERDICT: PASS -- the CR56263 crash regression did not reproduce. `mirror interface none`
         makes the port an ACL-driven mirror destination and removes cleanly, and NSM and
         the system were unaffected throughout.
      command accepted .................. yes      (no error)
      reported as ACL-driven ............ yes      (Monitored Port Name: none (via ACL))
      NSM process ....................... unchanged (pid 795, 12 threads, before and after)
      NSM restarts ...................... none     (an unchanged pid rules out crash+respawn)
      system reboots .................... none     (uptime monotonic 00:10:12 -> 00:12:09)
      crash/restart log entries ......... none
      dataplane during and after ........ serving  (ping 6/6, 0% loss)
      control plane during and after .... serving  (CLI answered, aggregation intact)
      removal ........................... clean    (no residue, NSM still pid 795)
CAVEAT:  This case is a crash regression, not a feature test, so it is graded on stability
         rather than on mirroring behaviour. `mirror interface none` is the ACL-driven
         destination form used by `copy-to-mirror`; it is not an "off" switch, and no
         ACL-driven mirroring was exercised here. Defects found elsewhere in this group mean
         mirror status output on this platform is not evidence of dataplane behaviour, so it
         is recorded below as observation only -- the verdict rests on the process, uptime,
         log and service evidence.

